  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,-\\frac{1}{2})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9\\,(\\frac{3}{4})+(y+2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{27}{4}+(y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{36}{4} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,\\,-\\frac{7}{2}).\n$$",
